  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nSurface high pressure settling into the area this morning has \nmade good radiational cooling conditions across the Northland. \nFog has already developed in a few of our more fog prone \nlocations this morning, and we are likely to see additional \ndevelopment through early Sunday morning. Any morning fog will \nquickly burn off after sunrise on Sunday, giving way to a very \npleasant and quiet late August day. The high pressure will \nproviding mostly sunny skies and light northwest winds. Minimum \nrelative humidity values will dip into the 30 to 40 percent \nrange during the afternoon hours. Tonight will feature another \nround of mostly clear skies and chilly overnight low \ntemperatures in the forties with a few locations possibly \ndipping into the upper 30s. \n\nLate Sunday night into Monday daytime, we will have a leading \nwave of elevated warm air advection and a warm front pass west \nto east across the area. Some models are producing showers and \nstorms with it, but the low level dry air appears to be too \nstout to allow any precipitation to reach the ground. Also, most\nmodels are completely dry for us on Monday, and have decided to\nleave the forecast dry for now. I do expect an increase in \ncloud cover and perhaps some elevated radar echoes, but that dry\nair is likely to keep the low levels dry. \n\nPrecipitation chances increase as the main upper level \nshortwave moves across the area late Monday night through \nWednesday. Monday night into early Tuesday we have some elevated\ninstability, so we will likely have some rumbles of thunder \novernight, but am not concerned for severe storms. We do have an\narea of Marginal severe risk just to our west from SPC, but \nsuspect this may shift farther west based on the latest models. \nIf we get any strong storms, they would be towards Tuesday \nmorning. Tuesday will be warm and more humid, with some surface \nbased instability developing during the afternoon and evening \nhours. Then, just as the upper level shortwave moves across the \narea and deep layer shear parameters increase, I expect another \nwave of showers and thunderstorms to move through the area, \nwhich will have the potential for strong to severe storms. While\nthings look interesting now, we will have to monitor for timing\nor other changes that may change this. Showers and non-severe \nstorms will redevelop across the area on Wednesday in the \ncyclonic flow behind the upper level shortwave. Wednesday and \nThursday will be cooler again. The cyclonic flow may help to \ngenerate isolated showers again on Thursday, though the incoming\ndry air may quash them. We currently have a dry forecast, but \nif we have a bit more moisture, that may change.\n\nHigh pressure and northwest flow aloft return for the latter\nportions of the work week, bringing a return to dry conditions \nacross the Northland for Thursday into Friday. Precipitation \nchances remain below fifteen percent through the end of the \nperiod.",
